00:08:02.230 --> 00:08:04.390
So like I had
mentioned, Andromeda

00:08:04.390 --> 00:08:08.240
is our network virtualization
for Google Cloud.

00:08:08.240 --> 00:08:11.120
What do I mean by
network virtualization?

00:08:11.120 --> 00:08:16.550
It essentially refers to having
multiple virtual networks that

00:08:16.550 --> 00:08:21.710
essentially run on top of
a shared physical network.

00:08:21.710 --> 00:08:24.860
On a virtual network, you
can have multiple VMs that

00:08:24.860 --> 00:08:27.090
essentially talk to each other.

00:08:27.090 --> 00:08:31.610
And these physical VMs in turn
run on a physical network.

00:08:31.610 --> 00:08:34.309
The IP addressing scheme
of a virtual network

00:08:34.309 --> 00:08:38.090
is totally decoupled
from a virtual network.

00:08:38.090 --> 00:08:42.049
Forwarding in virtual networks
is done by means of a vSwitch.

00:08:42.049 --> 00:08:46.820
A vSwitch essentially has a
mapping of the destination VM

00:08:46.820 --> 00:08:51.860
address to the actual
physical host's IP address.

00:08:51.860 --> 00:08:54.020
Packet forwarding
happens essentially

00:08:54.020 --> 00:08:57.740
like it does with all network
overlays, using encapsulation.

00:08:57.740 --> 00:09:00.980
You encapsulate the
customer's IP packet

00:09:00.980 --> 00:09:04.940
into another IP packet
with the transport header

00:09:04.940 --> 00:09:10.010
meant for the destination
IP address of the host.

00:09:10.010 --> 00:09:12.660
So now this is where
the fun stuff comes.

00:09:12.660 --> 00:09:15.500
What does Andromeda
architecture look like?

00:09:15.500 --> 00:09:17.690
The Andromeda
architecture is based

00:09:17.690 --> 00:09:21.860
on a hierarchy of control
planes and data planes.

00:09:21.860 --> 00:09:23.930
At the top of the
control plane hierarchy

00:09:23.930 --> 00:09:26.730
are our virtual
machine controllers.

00:09:26.730 --> 00:09:28.460
These virtual
machine controllers

00:09:28.460 --> 00:09:32.420
receive an abstract
representation of the network,

00:09:32.420 --> 00:09:36.110
specifying what are your QS
rules, what are your firewall

00:09:36.110 --> 00:09:39.710
rules, what are your routes,
what are your subnets, what

00:09:39.710 --> 00:09:42.500
are the VMS, et cetera.

00:09:42.500 --> 00:09:46.580
It then translates that
into open flow commands

00:09:46.580 --> 00:09:50.420
to the virtual switches
you see in the grade below.

00:09:50.420 --> 00:09:53.990
It does not directly communicate
with the virtual switches.

00:09:53.990 --> 00:09:58.010
But it goes through a proxy
called the open flow front end.

00:09:58.010 --> 00:10:02.490
These open flow front
ends serve two purposes.

00:10:02.490 --> 00:10:07.500
One, it serves to decouple
the open flow protocol

00:10:07.500 --> 00:10:10.380
from the Andromeda
control plane.

00:10:10.380 --> 00:10:12.540
It also has no state.

00:10:12.540 --> 00:10:16.260
It essentially just translates
the open flow config commands

00:10:16.260 --> 00:10:18.480
into open flow
protocol commands.

00:10:18.480 --> 00:10:20.640
Because it has no
state, it serves

00:10:20.640 --> 00:10:26.040
as a static connection point for
the vSwitches to the network.

00:10:26.040 --> 00:10:28.200
Because of that,
you can potentially

00:10:28.200 --> 00:10:31.380
upgrade the control plane
without affecting the vSwitches

00:10:31.380 --> 00:10:32.550
below.

00:10:32.550 --> 00:10:35.520
The vSwitches have
a control plane

00:10:35.520 --> 00:10:40.410
based on the standards-based
open flow vSwitch.

00:10:40.410 --> 00:10:42.750
But we've added
proprietary extensions

00:10:42.750 --> 00:10:46.800
for delivering some of
our network services.

00:10:46.800 --> 00:10:48.960
Now a key takeaway
from this slide

00:10:48.960 --> 00:10:53.790
is that our VMC controllers
are sharded, are partitioned.

00:10:53.790 --> 00:10:57.150
And that helps you get
scalability, right?

00:10:57.150 --> 00:10:59.040
If you want to bring
a new rack online,

00:10:59.040 --> 00:11:02.910
all you have to do is spin up
a new job for a virtual machine

00:11:02.910 --> 00:11:05.740
controller, some open flow
front ends, and voila,

00:11:05.740 --> 00:11:07.850
you're up and running.

00:11:07.850 --> 00:11:11.900
Each instance of a VMC
is also replicated.

00:11:11.900 --> 00:11:16.160
There are three instances,
one master, two standby,

00:11:16.160 --> 00:11:17.660
using the Paxos protocol.

00:11:17.660 --> 00:11:21.230
So having it sharded, I
mean having it replicated

00:11:21.230 --> 00:11:23.300
gives you high availability.

00:11:23.300 --> 00:11:28.160
So our whole control
plane is highly available

00:11:28.160 --> 00:11:29.860
and does scale out.

00:11:32.790 --> 00:11:34.840
Now moving on to
our data plane--

00:11:34.840 --> 00:11:37.710
if there's one thing I'd like
you to take away of our data

00:11:37.710 --> 00:11:41.310
plane, or what's unique
about our data plane,

00:11:41.310 --> 00:11:48.360
is that it is a very performant
OS-bypassed user space

00:11:48.360 --> 00:11:53.380
architecture running on
a dedicated CPU core.

00:11:53.380 --> 00:11:57.220
When I say fast, even though
it's running on user space,

00:11:57.220 --> 00:12:01.030
we can hit about 3 million
packets per second.

00:12:01.030 --> 00:12:05.890
It's crafted very optimally,
but taking advantage of things

00:12:05.890 --> 00:12:10.690
like memory locality avoiding,
synchronization like locks,

00:12:10.690 --> 00:12:13.210
and taking advantage
of large page sizes,

00:12:13.210 --> 00:12:16.630
and avoiding thread hand-offs,
and avoiding system calls,

00:12:16.630 --> 00:12:22.140
doing end-to-end batching,
all to make it extremely fast.

00:12:22.140 --> 00:12:24.320
Now it's also extremely secure.

00:12:24.320 --> 00:12:28.360
Remember, this is a process
running in user space.

00:12:28.360 --> 00:12:30.960
There is no root access.

00:12:30.960 --> 00:12:34.310
In addition, the code is so
constrained that its attack

00:12:34.310 --> 00:12:36.287
surface is also very limited.

00:12:36.287 --> 00:12:38.120
We also implement the
best-of-breed security

00:12:38.120 --> 00:12:42.770
practices, such as address-based
[INAUDIBLE] randomization

00:12:42.770 --> 00:12:46.280
and having stack canaries,
alter events, stack overflow.

00:12:46.280 --> 00:12:50.090
So net-net, it's an
extremely secure process.

00:12:50.090 --> 00:12:53.060
One point I should
mention is let's contrast

00:12:53.060 --> 00:12:56.960
this implementation of the data
plane with other alternatives.

00:12:56.960 --> 00:12:58.670
One thing you could
do with a data plane

00:12:58.670 --> 00:13:00.800
is implement it in a kernel.

00:13:00.800 --> 00:13:03.800
Or you could implement
it in hardware.

00:13:03.800 --> 00:13:06.020
The challenges with
those are, if you

00:13:06.020 --> 00:13:08.570
have a crash in the
data plane and a kernel,

00:13:08.570 --> 00:13:10.460
you take the whole system out.

00:13:10.460 --> 00:13:15.170
And, you know, god help you if
you have it in the hardware.

00:13:15.170 --> 00:13:18.450
Last but not the least,
it's also robust.

00:13:18.450 --> 00:13:21.300
When a process-- it's
a user space process,

00:13:21.300 --> 00:13:23.370
so you can restart it.

00:13:23.370 --> 00:13:25.260
The control plane just
detects that the user

00:13:25.260 --> 00:13:31.320
space has crashed, and you
can restart it very easily.

00:13:31.320 --> 00:13:34.280
In addition, we have
mechanisms by which

00:13:34.280 --> 00:13:38.120
you can upgrade, do a hitless
upgrade of the data plane

00:13:38.120 --> 00:13:38.990
process.

00:13:38.990 --> 00:13:44.360
So net-net, we can effectively
roll out a data plane change

00:13:44.360 --> 00:13:49.710
every week, be agile, and have
many rapid releases on the data

00:13:49.710 --> 00:13:52.000
plane.

00:13:52.000 --> 00:13:55.100
So with this control
and unique data plane,

00:13:55.100 --> 00:13:58.150
What are some of the network
services enabled by Andromeda?

00:13:58.150 --> 00:14:01.510
You, of course, mentioned
isolation as your requirement.

00:14:01.510 --> 00:14:05.020
We'll see some of these
services and how Andromeda

00:14:05.020 --> 00:14:06.850
enables some of these services.

00:14:06.850 --> 00:14:10.390
But two key takeaways
are, we make

00:14:10.390 --> 00:14:14.960
them very highly available,
and there is no chokepoint.

00:14:14.960 --> 00:14:19.390
It's an architecture that
scales out very well.

00:14:19.390 --> 00:14:23.030
So diving right in, how do
you get functional isolation?

00:14:23.030 --> 00:14:26.090
Functional isolation, it's
a standard overlay model,

00:14:26.090 --> 00:14:29.390
where you encapsulate
your customer IP

00:14:29.390 --> 00:14:33.740
packet and the other
outer IP packet,

00:14:33.740 --> 00:14:38.250
and essentially get
functional isolation that way.

00:14:38.250 --> 00:14:40.650
Performance
isolation-- you know,

00:14:40.650 --> 00:14:44.200
when I mentioned that the
data planes were hierarchical?

00:14:44.200 --> 00:14:46.200
The control and data
planes were hierarchical.

00:14:46.200 --> 00:14:48.720
Even the data plane
has the falling paths.

00:14:48.720 --> 00:14:50.520
We have the fastpath.

00:14:50.520 --> 00:14:53.160
This is for your
performance-critical path.

00:14:53.160 --> 00:14:56.530
It has fixed cycle budget.

00:14:56.530 --> 00:15:02.440
And there is isolation
by TX and RX for that.

00:15:02.440 --> 00:15:04.620
In addition, there are
certain networking features

00:15:04.620 --> 00:15:06.850
that are obviously
CPU-intensive that

00:15:06.850 --> 00:15:11.100
will take more than
necessary cycles than usual.

00:15:11.100 --> 00:15:14.340
For those we have the
CPU-intensive path.

00:15:14.340 --> 00:15:18.430
And the way we implement
them are per VM threads,

00:15:18.430 --> 00:15:21.330
CPU attributed to
the VM container

00:15:21.330 --> 00:15:25.480
for those more
CPU-intensive operations.

00:15:25.480 --> 00:15:29.650
This essentially allows us
to be more fair because it's

00:15:29.650 --> 00:15:34.060
a poor VM thread with attributes
for that fairly allocated

00:15:34.060 --> 00:15:35.980
for that container.

00:15:35.980 --> 00:15:38.620
In addition, having
the CPU-intensive path

00:15:38.620 --> 00:15:43.390
helps us to decouple the feature
velocity from the fastpath.

00:15:43.390 --> 00:15:47.020
So typically you are not
modifying the fastpath when

00:15:47.020 --> 00:15:48.730
you want to add new features.

00:15:48.730 --> 00:15:50.610
You typically add
new features that

00:15:50.610 --> 00:15:55.120
are more performance intensive
with the per VM thread, so you

00:15:55.120 --> 00:15:59.650
maintain both feature velocity
as well as not disrupt

00:15:59.650 --> 00:16:02.472
the core fastpath functionality.

00:16:02.472 --> 00:16:04.180
There's also something
called Hoverboard.

00:16:04.180 --> 00:16:06.340
I'll discuss this a little
further down the road.

00:16:06.340 --> 00:16:09.310
But it's essentially a
dedicated software gateway

00:16:09.310 --> 00:16:11.730
for processing the
long tail flows.

00:16:16.770 --> 00:16:19.560
Now before I-- I kind
of gave the answer away,

00:16:19.560 --> 00:16:23.130
but essentially, how many of
you remember the Spectre bug?

00:16:23.130 --> 00:16:24.030
Right?

00:16:24.030 --> 00:16:24.780
Mindmeld?

00:16:24.780 --> 00:16:28.470
So if you remember,
when we hit that bug,

00:16:28.470 --> 00:16:32.010
we were able to upgrade
our infrastructure

00:16:32.010 --> 00:16:35.280
and just let you guys,
our customers, our users,

00:16:35.280 --> 00:16:39.690
know that we had upgraded,
that there was an issue,

00:16:39.690 --> 00:16:41.080
we had upgraded it.

00:16:41.080 --> 00:16:42.780
And the way we were
able to upgrade

00:16:42.780 --> 00:16:45.540
the infrastructure
without disruption

00:16:45.540 --> 00:16:48.890
was by using live migration.

00:16:48.890 --> 00:16:50.040
What is live migration?

00:16:50.040 --> 00:16:51.690
It is, which is
essentially migrate

00:16:51.690 --> 00:16:55.620
one VM to another machine with
extremely minimal blackout

00:16:55.620 --> 00:16:56.480
period.

00:16:56.480 --> 00:16:58.270
Where does Andromeda
come into the play?

00:16:58.270 --> 00:17:01.230
Andromeda facilitates
live migration,

00:17:01.230 --> 00:17:03.960
even across clusters.

00:17:03.960 --> 00:17:06.780
If you step back a
little bit, right?

00:17:06.780 --> 00:17:10.920
If you think of a global routing
plane, and routing converges,

00:17:10.920 --> 00:17:12.720
enabling us to do--

00:17:12.720 --> 00:17:15.349
in the time it takes
for routing to converge,

00:17:15.349 --> 00:17:21.240
the time we're able to do this
VM migration across clusters.

00:17:21.240 --> 00:17:23.130
It's pretty impressive.

00:17:23.130 --> 00:17:27.240
And like I said, it's useful
for maintenance upgrades,

00:17:27.240 --> 00:17:29.460
optimizing the placement
of VMs, and of course,

00:17:29.460 --> 00:17:31.920
fixing the zero-day
bugs under the cover.

00:17:31.920 --> 00:17:35.100
The way Andromeda
does this is it

00:17:35.100 --> 00:17:38.940
essentially directs the
migration source host

00:17:38.940 --> 00:17:42.540
switch to help in all
traffic that it receives

00:17:42.540 --> 00:17:47.100
from other gateways and
hosts to the target switch

00:17:47.100 --> 00:17:50.340
during the blackout period.

00:17:50.340 --> 00:17:53.520
After the blackout
period ends, it then

00:17:53.520 --> 00:17:57.720
instructs all other
VMs and our gateways

00:17:57.720 --> 00:18:03.250
to then send traffic
to the target switch.

00:18:03.250 --> 00:18:07.180
I mean, this is a very
useful feature for us,

00:18:07.180 --> 00:18:13.370
maintaining high
availability and resiliency.

00:18:13.370 --> 00:18:15.440
Now going on to the
network services

00:18:15.440 --> 00:18:17.450
that I was referring to--

00:18:17.450 --> 00:18:19.640
internal load balancing,
it essentially

00:18:19.640 --> 00:18:22.780
helps you scale and
grow your services

00:18:22.780 --> 00:18:28.370
behind a virtual load
balancing IP address that

00:18:28.370 --> 00:18:31.940
are only accessible from
the back end instances.

00:18:31.940 --> 00:18:33.650
You know, inside
the Google network,

00:18:33.650 --> 00:18:35.600
inside your customer
network, it's all--

00:18:35.600 --> 00:18:38.390
I mean, it's mostly
RFC 1918 addresses.

00:18:38.390 --> 00:18:43.010
So you use an RFC 1918
VIP for this purpose.

00:18:43.010 --> 00:18:48.080
We support health checks, and
we preserve the client IP.

00:18:48.080 --> 00:18:52.480
What's important to note is
that internal load balancing

00:18:52.480 --> 00:18:53.860
has no middle proxy.

00:18:53.860 --> 00:19:00.010
There is nothing called an
actual internal load balancer.

00:19:00.010 --> 00:19:02.080
So traditionally you had a VIP.

00:19:02.080 --> 00:19:05.470
You had an actual proxy instance
that did the load balancing

00:19:05.470 --> 00:19:06.760
to your back ends.

00:19:06.760 --> 00:19:10.030
But the way GCP does this
internal load balancing,

00:19:10.030 --> 00:19:13.030
is that the load balancing
functionality is directly

00:19:13.030 --> 00:19:16.360
embedded and programmed
in the client VM itself.

00:19:16.360 --> 00:19:20.020
So the load balancing
decision happens

00:19:20.020 --> 00:19:21.910
in the client VM itself.

00:19:21.910 --> 00:19:28.010
And it then gets sent to
the appropriate back end.

00:19:28.010 --> 00:19:30.980
What this means is that we can
scale out our load balancer

00:19:30.980 --> 00:19:35.000
without having any chokepoints.

00:19:35.000 --> 00:19:38.090
The way it works is essentially
the controllers program

00:19:38.090 --> 00:19:41.420
the open flow rules
into the client VMs.

00:19:41.420 --> 00:19:44.120
It associates the back
end VMs with the VIP.

00:19:44.120 --> 00:19:47.180
The open flow, there are
certain proprietary extensions

00:19:47.180 --> 00:19:51.770
in terms of doing sticky load
balancing, because you have

00:19:51.770 --> 00:19:56.690
to do both connection tracking
as well as doing consistent

00:19:56.690 --> 00:20:00.080
hashing to essentially get the
load balancing function that

00:20:00.080 --> 00:20:04.250
is both scalable, fair, and
also resilient to outages.

00:20:04.250 --> 00:20:08.810
We support health checks that
track the state of the back end

00:20:08.810 --> 00:20:10.100
instances.

00:20:10.100 --> 00:20:13.520
And this is essentially what
the control plane for an ILB

00:20:13.520 --> 00:20:14.030
looks like.

00:20:16.850 --> 00:20:20.240
Now you have to connect on prem.

00:20:20.240 --> 00:20:24.560
Typically you use
either a physical router

00:20:24.560 --> 00:20:30.320
that has both support for
BGP and VPN functionality.

00:20:30.320 --> 00:20:33.800
And the BGP portion
of that gateway

00:20:33.800 --> 00:20:35.720
learns your on-prem routes.

00:20:35.720 --> 00:20:39.320
And then the data
plane of that gateway

00:20:39.320 --> 00:20:42.920
encrypts it using IPsec,
VPN, and then sends it

00:20:42.920 --> 00:20:44.720
across the tunnel.

00:20:44.720 --> 00:20:46.320
We took a slightly
different approach.

00:20:46.320 --> 00:20:48.710
We could have, of course,
just deployed this instance.

00:20:48.710 --> 00:20:53.000
But in order to make
your router both scale

00:20:53.000 --> 00:20:59.630
and make it highly available, we
actually took the routing piece

00:20:59.630 --> 00:21:01.550
and made it a separate job.

00:21:01.550 --> 00:21:05.660
A lot of our implementations
are essentially just,

00:21:05.660 --> 00:21:09.320
you can think of it as
jobs running in our fabric.

00:21:09.320 --> 00:21:14.210
This cloud router job
peers with the BGP on prem,

00:21:14.210 --> 00:21:18.080
learns her routes,
and then it programs

00:21:18.080 --> 00:21:20.690
those routes to the
VM, telling them

00:21:20.690 --> 00:21:24.260
that in order to reach
your customer data center,

00:21:24.260 --> 00:21:28.580
send the packets
directly to the VM.

00:21:28.580 --> 00:21:30.130
So what does this buy you?

00:21:30.130 --> 00:21:35.930
You essentially get a separation
of the control and data plane.

00:21:35.930 --> 00:21:37.760
Hence, you get more resiliency.

00:21:42.060 --> 00:21:44.160
When I talk about high
availability, what

00:21:44.160 --> 00:21:47.280
this translates to
is that you have,

00:21:47.280 --> 00:21:51.000
essentially, VPN
jobs or router jobs

00:21:51.000 --> 00:21:53.230
that are available in a pool.

00:21:53.230 --> 00:21:57.600
When we have a command to
create a VPN or create a router,

00:21:57.600 --> 00:22:01.170
the Config Manager
essentially instructs,

00:22:01.170 --> 00:22:03.270
let's say for our router,
the routing manager

00:22:03.270 --> 00:22:07.140
to allocate a router from
this pool of router tasks

00:22:07.140 --> 00:22:10.560
and assigns them to that job.

00:22:10.560 --> 00:22:13.470
The routing manager then
talks to the fabric manager

00:22:13.470 --> 00:22:17.820
and the Andromeda control plane,
setting up the associations

00:22:17.820 --> 00:22:22.800
between the router and the VMs.

00:22:22.800 --> 00:22:25.770
Now should a router
process crash,

00:22:25.770 --> 00:22:30.240
it's fine because the data plane
continues to forward packets

00:22:30.240 --> 00:22:33.510
as before till
the BGP hold time.

00:22:33.510 --> 00:22:37.950
The routing manager, which is
tracking the routing instance,

00:22:37.950 --> 00:22:40.710
detects that the current
router has gone away,

00:22:40.710 --> 00:22:44.720
spins up a new routing instance,
and essentially re-establishes

00:22:44.720 --> 00:22:45.960
a relationship.

00:22:45.960 --> 00:22:49.890
So you have high
availability and no downtime,

00:22:49.890 --> 00:22:52.450
even if the control plane
of the router crashes.

00:22:55.920 --> 00:22:58.860
Similar fashion,
think of firewalls.

00:22:58.860 --> 00:23:02.520
Typically, firewalls have been
deployed at the perimeter.

00:23:02.520 --> 00:23:04.950
There are potentially
chokepoints.

00:23:04.950 --> 00:23:10.030
And they didn't scale very well.

00:23:10.030 --> 00:23:14.710
With Andromeda architecture,
our firewall rules

00:23:14.710 --> 00:23:17.830
are actually deployed directly
on our compute engines,

00:23:17.830 --> 00:23:19.990
the ingress and the egress.

00:23:19.990 --> 00:23:21.205
There is connection tracking.

00:23:24.140 --> 00:23:27.190
And that's the way
we are able to scale,

00:23:27.190 --> 00:23:28.960
because there is no chokepoint.

00:23:28.960 --> 00:23:31.990
All the firewall rules
are deployed directly

00:23:31.990 --> 00:23:34.690
on the compute engines per se.

00:23:34.690 --> 00:23:38.130
So to summarize, there
is no chokepoint,

00:23:38.130 --> 00:23:40.720
it's stateful with
connection tracking.

00:23:40.720 --> 00:23:43.030
And we also support
flexible grouping mechanisms

00:23:43.030 --> 00:23:45.980
for tags and service accounts.

00:23:45.980 --> 00:23:48.690
So now we have seen
services such as firewalls,

00:23:48.690 --> 00:23:57.100
load balancers, and routers
being deployed using Andromeda.

00:23:57.100 --> 00:23:58.900
I'm going to segue
into a new topic,

00:23:58.900 --> 00:24:00.440
but that's very relevant.

00:24:00.440 --> 00:24:02.580
How do you scale with Andromeda?

00:24:02.580 --> 00:24:04.420
Remember when I had
made a reference

00:24:04.420 --> 00:24:06.640
to it being a global network?

00:24:06.640 --> 00:24:09.040
Well global networks are big.

00:24:09.040 --> 00:24:13.090
You also have to remember
that a lot of our users

00:24:13.090 --> 00:24:17.710
are Fortune 100 clients and a
lot of large internet companies

00:24:17.710 --> 00:24:21.380
with very large
internet-based workloads.

00:24:21.380 --> 00:24:27.340
So there are asks for a
network having 100K-plus VMs

00:24:27.340 --> 00:24:31.670
in addition to
having more networks.

00:24:31.670 --> 00:24:33.940
Another point to note
is that it's not just

00:24:33.940 --> 00:24:37.810
the size of the network
or the number of networks.

00:24:37.810 --> 00:24:39.670
There's also the
concern of how do you

00:24:39.670 --> 00:24:43.370
provision a large network
really, really fast?

00:24:43.370 --> 00:24:47.510
So if you take of use
cases, such as MapReduce

00:24:47.510 --> 00:24:52.135
our TensorFlow, there is a need
to provision a really large

00:24:52.135 --> 00:24:54.770
network really, really fast.

00:24:54.770 --> 00:24:57.230
How do you do that?

00:24:57.230 --> 00:25:01.120
So the way we do that is
with using Hoverboards.

00:25:01.120 --> 00:25:06.262
Hoverboards are, in
essence, a software gateway.

00:25:06.262 --> 00:25:07.720
If you come from
the routing world,

00:25:07.720 --> 00:25:11.230
think of it as a top
of rack [INAUDIBLE]..

00:25:11.230 --> 00:25:13.630
I mean, think of it as a
route reflector, a BGP route

00:25:13.630 --> 00:25:14.500
reflector.

00:25:14.500 --> 00:25:16.041
If you come from
the switching world,

00:25:16.041 --> 00:25:19.540
think of it as a top of rack
switch, which is essentially

00:25:19.540 --> 00:25:24.070
forwarding information
for all the flows.

00:25:24.070 --> 00:25:27.290
Of course, it's a dedicated
software instance.

00:25:27.290 --> 00:25:30.210
You can have larger
forwarding tables.

00:25:30.210 --> 00:25:33.252
The virtual switches
themselves have essentially--

00:25:33.252 --> 00:25:34.460
you know, they're a default--

00:25:34.460 --> 00:25:36.250
the Hoverboard's
a default gateway.

00:25:36.250 --> 00:25:39.160
Default routes from the
virtual switch itself

00:25:39.160 --> 00:25:42.500
to the Hoverboard,
very standard.

00:25:42.500 --> 00:25:46.020
So with this way, you can
scale your control pane.

00:25:46.020 --> 00:25:49.790
The reason programming time is
not significant, because see,

00:25:49.790 --> 00:25:53.780
all your programming is a small
subset of Hoverboard as opposed

00:25:53.780 --> 00:25:55.520
to programming every switch.

00:25:55.520 --> 00:25:57.440
As you can see, the
order of difference

00:25:57.440 --> 00:26:00.190
in terms of programming time.

00:26:00.190 --> 00:26:03.970
Last but not the least, it's
important to note that this,

00:26:03.970 --> 00:26:06.670
if you're getting control
plane scalability,

00:26:06.670 --> 00:26:08.200
there's a trade-off
in data plane

00:26:08.200 --> 00:26:11.950
scalability because
you are having

00:26:11.950 --> 00:26:15.970
to provision your data plane to
deal with the peak bandwidth.

00:26:15.970 --> 00:26:17.470
You have to provision
the Hoverboard

00:26:17.470 --> 00:26:21.200
to deal with that peak
bandwidth of all the flows.

00:26:21.200 --> 00:26:23.560
So how do we get
around that requirement

00:26:23.560 --> 00:26:26.470
for supporting the peak
bandwidth of all the flows

00:26:26.470 --> 00:26:28.390
that has to go through
the Hoverboard?

00:26:28.390 --> 00:26:31.120
Well, the way they do
it is we essentially

00:26:31.120 --> 00:26:34.510
offload some
high-bandwidth flows

00:26:34.510 --> 00:26:37.630
to the virtual switches
directly, right?

00:26:37.630 --> 00:26:40.600
So we essentially program.

00:26:40.600 --> 00:26:43.300
We detect that a flow
is very high bandwidth.

00:26:43.300 --> 00:26:46.180
And we program it directly
to go from the virtual switch

00:26:46.180 --> 00:26:50.400
to the virtual switch itself
and offload the Hoverboard.

00:26:50.400 --> 00:26:55.090
In this way, we get both control
plane scalability as well as

00:26:55.090 --> 00:26:58.630
data plane scalability.

00:26:58.630 --> 00:27:00.030
But how does this work, really?

00:27:00.030 --> 00:27:01.870
Why does it work?

00:27:01.870 --> 00:27:06.790
We went and studied
all the network flows

00:27:06.790 --> 00:27:10.060
in our data center, all
the east-west flows.

00:27:10.060 --> 00:27:13.600
And we noticed is that
most VM pairs don't really

00:27:13.600 --> 00:27:15.040
talk to each other.

00:27:15.040 --> 00:27:17.800
And very few, if they
do talk to each other,

00:27:17.800 --> 00:27:20.020
have very low
bandwidth requirements.

00:27:20.020 --> 00:27:22.450
It's only an
extremely small amount

00:27:22.450 --> 00:27:26.500
that has really high
bandwidth flows amongst them.

00:27:26.500 --> 00:27:30.490
So those are the ones we
offload from the Hoverboard

00:27:30.490 --> 00:27:32.970
to the vSwitches
themselves, right?

00:27:35.630 --> 00:27:39.830
Now going back to just this
point about why does it take--

00:27:39.830 --> 00:27:42.680
is it quicker to provision
the Hoverboards, right?

00:27:42.680 --> 00:27:45.920
Because you have only a
small subset of the software

00:27:45.920 --> 00:27:47.960
switches that you
have to provision.

00:27:47.960 --> 00:27:52.670
So the programming time
is nice and linear.

00:27:52.670 --> 00:27:56.600
But think about preprogramming
all the virtual switches

00:27:56.600 --> 00:27:58.400
with all the forwarding rules.

00:27:58.400 --> 00:28:02.630
It essentially starts
out as a quadratic growth

00:28:02.630 --> 00:28:05.360
in terms of programming time.

00:28:05.360 --> 00:28:09.800
But slowly, if you are in a
specific zone and a lot of VMs

00:28:09.800 --> 00:28:13.820
share the same physical
host, the programming time

00:28:13.820 --> 00:28:15.170
then becomes linear.

00:28:15.170 --> 00:28:16.430
But it's too late.

00:28:16.430 --> 00:28:19.620
I mean, you've already
exceeded the programming time

00:28:19.620 --> 00:28:21.170
with the quadratic growth.

00:28:21.170 --> 00:28:23.390
So effectively,
with Hoverboards,

00:28:23.390 --> 00:28:27.650
we are able to see a 37%
improvement in programming

00:28:27.650 --> 00:28:31.300
time for large networks.

00:28:31.300 --> 00:28:35.020
We've started implementing
routing features

00:28:35.020 --> 00:28:36.250
on the Hoverboard.

00:28:36.250 --> 00:28:40.210
Additional features,
such as load balancing

00:28:40.210 --> 00:28:45.210
and other firewall features
are in progress of development.

00:28:45.210 --> 00:28:46.880
Now I'm going to
hand it over to Oz,

00:28:46.880 --> 00:28:50.341
who's going to show you
a real ILB in action.

00:28:53.950 --> 00:28:56.570
OSVALDO COSTA: In this demo
here that we have prepared,

00:28:56.570 --> 00:29:00.430
the idea is to show you guys
a real use case with some

00:29:00.430 --> 00:29:02.630
of the features that
Babi was covering

00:29:02.630 --> 00:29:04.630
and delivered using Andromeda.

00:29:04.630 --> 00:29:08.150
So we have an automated
deployment with multiple VPC

00:29:08.150 --> 00:29:09.730
networks, as you
guys can see here,

00:29:09.730 --> 00:29:14.290
so three VPC networks--
one called DMZ, another one

00:29:14.290 --> 00:29:16.660
Prod, another one Dev.

00:29:16.660 --> 00:29:23.380
Those VPCs are being connected
by multi-NIC Linux firewalls.

00:29:23.380 --> 00:29:28.240
And in those VPC, there are
also internal load balancers

00:29:28.240 --> 00:29:31.870
attached to managed instance
groups doing auto-scaling.

00:29:31.870 --> 00:29:34.390
So with this demo here,
what we are going to show

00:29:34.390 --> 00:29:39.010
is the auto-scaler in
action, showing the ILB

00:29:39.010 --> 00:29:42.260
with the preserved client
IP that Babi mentioned.

00:29:42.260 --> 00:29:44.140
So there is no
proxy in the middle.

00:29:44.140 --> 00:29:49.430
So connections from the clients
go directly to the servers.

00:29:49.430 --> 00:29:52.270
We are also going to show the
service discovery, the DNS

00:29:52.270 --> 00:29:54.820
name, for the ILB,
which is a new feature,

00:29:54.820 --> 00:29:57.880
and how to monitor all of
that with the Stackdriver.

00:30:04.700 --> 00:30:08.790
So here in the beginning--

00:30:08.790 --> 00:30:10.190
let me just increase this here.

00:30:15.880 --> 00:30:18.990
So what we are going
to show is first

00:30:18.990 --> 00:30:23.160
the network being defined,
so the three networks

00:30:23.160 --> 00:30:28.060
that I mentioned, Dev, Prod,
and DMZ with different segments.

00:30:28.060 --> 00:30:31.820
Now defining the fire rules,
we try to find at the VPC level

00:30:31.820 --> 00:30:33.910
but enforce at the
instance level.

00:30:33.910 --> 00:30:37.800
So one of the rules, the last
one, are the health checks.

00:30:37.800 --> 00:30:39.810
The are required for the ILB.

00:30:39.810 --> 00:30:41.610
And next the instances
that we have--

00:30:41.610 --> 00:30:44.670
so we have the bastion that we
are going to use for access,

00:30:44.670 --> 00:30:49.320
the load test VMs to generate
traffic, and a single web

00:30:49.320 --> 00:30:51.344
server in this first
step, just to show

00:30:51.344 --> 00:30:54.010
you guys the performance that we
are going to generate and blast

00:30:54.010 --> 00:30:55.080
that VM.

00:30:55.080 --> 00:30:56.880
And so just to show
you here, there

00:30:56.880 --> 00:31:00.720
are two MIGs, both of them
with auto-scaling off.

00:31:00.720 --> 00:31:04.110
And the web server MIG, there
is just a single instance,

00:31:04.110 --> 00:31:05.990
auto-scaling off, so a single--

00:31:05.990 --> 00:31:08.252
it's not going to auto-scale.

00:31:11.150 --> 00:31:13.990
Next what we are
going to show here

00:31:13.990 --> 00:31:17.680
is a start providing
access, just

00:31:17.680 --> 00:31:19.580
to show that this is
a live environment.

00:31:19.580 --> 00:31:24.400
So the real IP, for example,
for this first Linux firewall

00:31:24.400 --> 00:31:26.434
is getting through the
GCLB and then going

00:31:26.434 --> 00:31:27.350
to the Linux firewall.

00:31:27.350 --> 00:31:29.510
So this is the
nice landing page.

00:31:29.510 --> 00:31:32.300
One thing that I just wanted
to highlight, it's doing nat.

00:31:32.300 --> 00:31:35.050
So that's why you can you
do not see the XFF there,

00:31:35.050 --> 00:31:37.480
but you do see the
real client IP.

00:31:37.480 --> 00:31:41.020
Now there is a GCLB configured
to deliver this service as

00:31:41.020 --> 00:31:42.040
well.

00:31:42.040 --> 00:31:44.710
So the GCLB, the
external IP that

00:31:44.710 --> 00:31:47.350
is providing the load
balancing across the firewalls,

00:31:47.350 --> 00:31:50.260
if we hit it, you guys will
see a difference, which

00:31:50.260 --> 00:31:51.970
is the X-Forwarded-For Header.

00:31:51.970 --> 00:31:56.182
Because the GCLB is a
proxy-based service.

00:31:56.182 --> 00:31:57.640
So that's the main
difference, just

00:31:57.640 --> 00:32:01.415
to show you guys that
both services are--

00:32:01.415 --> 00:32:04.720
the services are available
through both, the GCLB

00:32:04.720 --> 00:32:06.790
and the direct connected IP.

00:32:06.790 --> 00:32:11.530
Now we are going to connect
here to the bastion host

00:32:11.530 --> 00:32:14.410
and start basically two
connections, one to the web

00:32:14.410 --> 00:32:16.450
server, and another
one to the load test.

00:32:16.450 --> 00:32:19.360
So we can generate
traffic and show you what

00:32:19.360 --> 00:32:20.640
a single instance can deliver.

00:32:28.210 --> 00:32:31.120
One important
thing also to note,

00:32:31.120 --> 00:32:34.240
and I'll highlight
here during the demo,

00:32:34.240 --> 00:32:35.980
is I have two web
servers loaded.

00:32:35.980 --> 00:32:38.110
So one of them is
showing, responding

00:32:38.110 --> 00:32:39.990
to port 80, which
is the one that you

00:32:39.990 --> 00:32:45.400
saw there, which is a very,
very small page, so low request.

00:32:45.400 --> 00:32:50.780
And the other one is 8080,
which is running a high process.

00:32:50.780 --> 00:32:52.900
So here, I'm just
showing a command just

00:32:52.900 --> 00:32:56.770
to show you guys that
this VM is programmed

00:32:56.770 --> 00:33:00.550
to respond to packets destined
to the ILB, which is the 10 0 1

00:33:00.550 --> 00:33:02.770
10, as you guys can see there.

00:33:02.770 --> 00:33:06.730
So that's how we can show
that the IP, the packets, they

00:33:06.730 --> 00:33:08.440
are delivered to the server.

00:33:08.440 --> 00:33:10.180
They reach directly the server.

00:33:10.180 --> 00:33:15.892
They do not flow through
any middle layer.

00:33:15.892 --> 00:33:18.350
Now we are going to show here
the servers that I mentioned.

00:33:18.350 --> 00:33:20.140
So there are two
servers, one listening

00:33:20.140 --> 00:33:22.080
on 8080, another one on 80.

00:33:22.080 --> 00:33:26.355
On 80 it's that simple
landing page that I showed.

00:33:26.355 --> 00:33:29.750
And on 8080, it's
actually a fractal.

00:33:29.750 --> 00:33:32.980
I started here a CPU
utilization monitor.

00:33:32.980 --> 00:33:36.280
As you can see, there are
pretty much no utilization.

00:33:36.280 --> 00:33:38.730
And I'll generate some traffic.

00:33:38.730 --> 00:33:41.200
Since this web server
is running a fractal,

00:33:41.200 --> 00:33:43.030
so there is a lot
of CPU utilization.

00:33:43.030 --> 00:33:45.880
So I'll generate two
sessions actually,

00:33:45.880 --> 00:33:47.040
which is here the command.

00:33:47.040 --> 00:33:50.540
So it will be 20 sessions
with a rate of two per second.

00:33:50.540 --> 00:33:53.920
And so we can see that the
CPU is spiking close to 85%.

00:33:57.120 --> 00:34:01.050
And again, this is using a
very CPU-intensive server.

00:34:01.050 --> 00:34:08.409
So once we do that, we are going
now back to the MIG to show--

00:34:08.409 --> 00:34:10.860
to change the config
of the MIG so we

00:34:10.860 --> 00:34:12.113
can show the auto-scaling.

00:34:15.909 --> 00:34:19.510
And just before that, if
I issue here the command

00:34:19.510 --> 00:34:23.510
with the watch, so we can take
a look at the open connections

00:34:23.510 --> 00:34:26.270
and generate the
sessions again, then

00:34:26.270 --> 00:34:30.739
we are going to see that
the commands, targeting now

00:34:30.739 --> 00:34:33.020
the ILB.

00:34:33.020 --> 00:34:37.400
So the first is starting the
real IP, so the IP from 0.5

00:34:37.400 --> 00:34:39.260
targeting the dot six.

00:34:39.260 --> 00:34:41.540
The next one that is
going to be done here

00:34:41.540 --> 00:34:44.060
is generating traffic
targeting the ILB

00:34:44.060 --> 00:34:46.610
address, which is this DNS
here being highlighted.

00:34:46.610 --> 00:34:48.530
So that's the DNS
entry that was created,

00:34:48.530 --> 00:34:52.010
that was defined when
creating the ILB.

00:34:52.010 --> 00:34:55.639
So sending packets
to that ILB, then we

00:34:55.639 --> 00:34:57.525
can see that,
actually, the packet

00:34:57.525 --> 00:34:59.650
monitor that will be done
here in the [INAUDIBLE]..

00:34:59.650 --> 00:35:02.570
So first a curl, just to
make sure that that ILB

00:35:02.570 --> 00:35:04.370
is responding to the traffic.

00:35:04.370 --> 00:35:07.460
And now when I start
generating that traffic,

00:35:07.460 --> 00:35:11.720
then we'll be able to see
in the connection tracking

00:35:11.720 --> 00:35:16.340
that the target IP is 10
0 1 10, which is the ILB.

00:35:16.340 --> 00:35:21.314
So that VM, once it's part of
a managed instance group tied

00:35:21.314 --> 00:35:22.730
to the internal
load balancing, is

00:35:22.730 --> 00:35:27.020
programmed to respond to packets
destined directly to the ILB.

00:35:27.020 --> 00:35:30.150
And one thing to highlight here
is that the number of sessions,

00:35:30.150 --> 00:35:32.540
which was around 2,000
connections per second,

00:35:32.540 --> 00:35:35.360
was the same number either
targeting directly the web

00:35:35.360 --> 00:35:37.250
server or targeting the ILB.

00:35:37.250 --> 00:35:40.890
So there is no
chokepoint in the middle.

00:35:40.890 --> 00:35:44.660
So now we are going to
configure the instance group

00:35:44.660 --> 00:35:47.780
and increase the
max size, actually

00:35:47.780 --> 00:35:49.360
enable the auto-scaler.

00:35:49.360 --> 00:35:54.080
So we go to the MIG, Edit,
the Managing Sys Group,

00:35:54.080 --> 00:35:59.600
enable auto-scaling, and set the
target CPU utilization to 80%,

00:35:59.600 --> 00:36:01.760
increase the number
of instances to six,

00:36:01.760 --> 00:36:03.140
and reduce the cool down.

00:36:03.140 --> 00:36:06.050
So cool down period is
the number of seconds

00:36:06.050 --> 00:36:09.309
before the instance is seen
as ready to receive traffic.

00:36:09.309 --> 00:36:10.850
So we're going to
have to change that

00:36:10.850 --> 00:36:13.490
just so it improves our demo.

00:36:13.490 --> 00:36:15.932
But in real life, you
should monitor that

00:36:15.932 --> 00:36:17.390
and see the amount
of time that you

00:36:17.390 --> 00:36:22.110
need before your instance
is ready to receive traffic.

00:36:22.110 --> 00:36:24.910
Now I have started
the CPU utilization,

00:36:24.910 --> 00:36:28.230
just to show here that
it was changed already.

00:36:28.230 --> 00:36:30.210
So it's ready to
receive traffic.

00:36:30.210 --> 00:36:32.880
And if you take a look at the
monitoring in the console,

00:36:32.880 --> 00:36:35.310
we can see that there
is one instance.

00:36:35.310 --> 00:36:38.730
And the total CPU
usage will be available

00:36:38.730 --> 00:36:41.190
as-- it will take a
few seconds, but will

00:36:41.190 --> 00:36:46.500
be available as 80%, which is
the threshold that we have set.

00:36:46.500 --> 00:36:51.460
Once I start generating
the traffic, which is here,

00:36:51.460 --> 00:36:57.920
so 1,200 sessions with a
rate of four per second, then

00:36:57.920 --> 00:37:01.000
I'll blast the CPU
for this machine.

00:37:01.000 --> 00:37:04.150
And we'll have to wait a little
bit until the MIG kicks in.

00:37:04.150 --> 00:37:06.930
So now switching
to Stackdriver, I

00:37:06.930 --> 00:37:09.810
have created this
custom dashboard that

00:37:09.810 --> 00:37:13.260
will show the per VM CPU
utilization plus all the ILB

00:37:13.260 --> 00:37:15.420
statistics, so we can monitor.

00:37:15.420 --> 00:37:18.480
But also, if we go here
directly to the instances,

00:37:18.480 --> 00:37:20.520
we can see a list
of all the instances

00:37:20.520 --> 00:37:23.129
that's going to load here
with the CPU utilization.

00:37:23.129 --> 00:37:25.170
Now we are seeing just
one, because of course, it

00:37:25.170 --> 00:37:28.250
takes few seconds
until the MIG kicks in,

00:37:28.250 --> 00:37:32.350
the new instances are created,
and the CPU is loaded.

00:37:32.350 --> 00:37:34.650
We can also see
this new feature.

00:37:34.650 --> 00:37:36.960
I don't know if
you guys are aware.

00:37:36.960 --> 00:37:40.140
But in Stackdriver, we
have loaded several metrics

00:37:40.140 --> 00:37:44.250
and available already in
Stackdriver for the ILB.

00:37:44.250 --> 00:37:45.780
So we can see all
the throughput,

00:37:45.780 --> 00:37:50.080
the packets, inbound,
outbound, latency

00:37:50.080 --> 00:37:53.670
for doing a breakdown by
instance group, for example,

00:37:53.670 --> 00:37:56.754
by server, or showing
all the key metrics here.

00:37:56.754 --> 00:37:58.920
So it's everything available
already in Stackdriver.

00:37:58.920 --> 00:37:59.780
This is built in.

00:37:59.780 --> 00:38:02.610
There is nothing that needs
to be customized to see this.

00:38:02.610 --> 00:38:05.910
And then you can break down
by several parameters here,

00:38:05.910 --> 00:38:09.480
several options, and
then take a look at it.

00:38:09.480 --> 00:38:12.650
Similarly, just to
show you guys here,

00:38:12.650 --> 00:38:15.180
we also have metrics
available for GCLB.

00:38:15.180 --> 00:38:17.170
It's not the focus
of this presentation.

00:38:17.170 --> 00:38:20.530
We have others specifically
covering the Google Cloud Load

00:38:20.530 --> 00:38:21.030
Balancer.

00:38:21.030 --> 00:38:24.330
But so you see, there
are several metrics

00:38:24.330 --> 00:38:26.470
are the built in
into Stackdriver

00:38:26.470 --> 00:38:30.650
to allow monitoring.

00:38:30.650 --> 00:38:35.180
Now what I have done is
build a custom dashboard

00:38:35.180 --> 00:38:38.450
to monitor all my main servers.

00:38:38.450 --> 00:38:43.010
So I'm grouping all servers
on a name by prod MIG,

00:38:43.010 --> 00:38:47.990
and also all the
ILB-related statistics.

00:38:47.990 --> 00:38:50.967
So we can see here
that the one VM

00:38:50.967 --> 00:38:52.550
that the Stackdriver
is seeing already

00:38:52.550 --> 00:38:54.200
has a spike here on CPU.

00:38:54.200 --> 00:38:56.420
It has a high CPU utilization.

00:38:56.420 --> 00:39:01.190
So once we go back now to the
console, we can take a look

00:39:01.190 --> 00:39:06.470
and see if the status of the MIG
and if it's increasing already.

00:39:10.250 --> 00:39:11.260
So doing a reload--

00:39:14.880 --> 00:39:17.250
and we can see
here that, not yet,

00:39:17.250 --> 00:39:20.100
but the members are
actually being spun up.

00:39:20.100 --> 00:39:24.440
So just one thing, the
VMs are being spun up.

00:39:24.440 --> 00:39:27.300
Soon they will be ready,
after the cool down period,

00:39:27.300 --> 00:39:28.980
after those 15 seconds.

00:39:28.980 --> 00:39:31.260
And they will be ready
to receive traffic.

00:39:31.260 --> 00:39:33.510
So once they are ready
to receive traffic,

00:39:33.510 --> 00:39:36.500
after it's up and running
in those 15 seconds,

00:39:36.500 --> 00:39:38.820
the VM is reported as running.

00:39:38.820 --> 00:39:41.550
And after those 15 seconds,
they're configurable.

00:39:41.550 --> 00:39:44.250
Then we are going to see
the traffic being loaded,

00:39:44.250 --> 00:39:46.170
distributed to all the VMs.

00:39:46.170 --> 00:39:51.270
And we'll see the VM utilization
for this first one being

00:39:51.270 --> 00:39:55.060
reduced, so be distributed.

00:39:55.060 --> 00:39:56.560
So it takes a few seconds.

00:39:56.560 --> 00:40:00.750
So also, so you guys know, the
MIG is pretty quick to kick in.

00:40:00.750 --> 00:40:04.320
However, it takes into
account the last 10 minutes

00:40:04.320 --> 00:40:06.170
of VM utilization
to take decisions

00:40:06.170 --> 00:40:07.870
to scale up or scale down.

00:40:07.870 --> 00:40:13.020
So that's why it takes a
few minutes to actually make

00:40:13.020 --> 00:40:15.470
the decision.

00:40:15.470 --> 00:40:17.000
But now you guys can see.

00:40:17.000 --> 00:40:22.300
So it has increased already to
240%, I believe, to three CPUs.

00:40:22.300 --> 00:40:26.310
So if we reload here, we'll
be able to see the CPU

00:40:26.310 --> 00:40:28.960
information and utilization.

00:40:28.960 --> 00:40:33.640
So the VMs are already
loaded here and available

00:40:33.640 --> 00:40:37.940
with the CPU utilization soon.

00:40:37.940 --> 00:40:42.680
And now back to the GCP
console, once you refresh,

00:40:42.680 --> 00:40:47.810
we are going to see
this utilization growing

00:40:47.810 --> 00:40:52.120
to actually 480%,
which would be the six

00:40:52.120 --> 00:40:55.280
VMs that I have
configured times 80%

00:40:55.280 --> 00:40:57.294
of available CPU utilization.

00:41:06.010 --> 00:41:10.900
So here in this demo, I'm
using the CPU as a metric

00:41:10.900 --> 00:41:12.880
to provide the auto-scaling.

00:41:12.880 --> 00:41:16.870
But you could also use, for
example, custom Stackdriver

00:41:16.870 --> 00:41:18.970
metrics to do this auto-scaling.

00:41:18.970 --> 00:41:20.860
So Stackdriver supports
custom metrics.

00:41:20.860 --> 00:41:22.120
You can build on your own.

00:41:22.120 --> 00:41:24.790
You can install
agents on your servers

00:41:24.790 --> 00:41:28.440
to monitor the parameters that
are more important for you.

00:41:28.440 --> 00:41:29.620
And then scale up.

00:41:29.620 --> 00:41:31.630
Based on that, scale
up and scale down.

00:41:31.630 --> 00:41:36.880
So now, as you can see here,
we can see all the VMs ready

00:41:36.880 --> 00:41:42.280
and utilization of
200% out of 240%,

00:41:42.280 --> 00:41:44.710
which are three VMs times 80.

00:41:44.710 --> 00:41:49.060
And now we see that the CPU
utilization in the first VM

00:41:49.060 --> 00:41:51.250
is reduced because
now the traffic is

00:41:51.250 --> 00:41:54.310
being spread across more VMs.

00:41:54.310 --> 00:41:59.670
So I'll do some refresh here
to see the updated information.

00:41:59.670 --> 00:42:04.750
Now I think in the next one,
all the VMs will be ready.

00:42:04.750 --> 00:42:07.960
And we'll see 480% CPU
utilization available

00:42:07.960 --> 00:42:10.210
that could be used.

00:42:10.210 --> 00:42:12.970
We'll then do a sort of a--

00:42:12.970 --> 00:42:15.430
I will do a fast
forward just because it

00:42:15.430 --> 00:42:19.530
takes 10 minutes, taking into
account the last 10 minutes

00:42:19.530 --> 00:42:20.380
to scale down.

00:42:20.380 --> 00:42:22.960
So you guys can see that
after traffic is stabilized,

00:42:22.960 --> 00:42:24.880
I stopped generating
traffic here.

00:42:24.880 --> 00:42:28.000
Then we'll see the utilization
going down again and then

00:42:28.000 --> 00:42:30.540
scaling down the servers
and back to normal life.

00:42:33.850 --> 00:42:36.200
And just to show
you here what it

00:42:36.200 --> 00:42:37.910
would look like in Stackdriver.

00:42:37.910 --> 00:42:40.130
So in Stackdriver, it
takes a few seconds

00:42:40.130 --> 00:42:43.980
to get that information
and load in the graph.

00:42:43.980 --> 00:42:46.680
So it's starting to
load here and create

00:42:46.680 --> 00:42:52.540
a nice graph for you of all the
CPU utilization and the data.

00:42:59.000 --> 00:43:03.150
So now the capacity is
480% and reduced to 120%

00:43:03.150 --> 00:43:07.210
utilization average.

00:43:07.210 --> 00:43:10.230
So one thing that is important,
this CPU utilization that we

00:43:10.230 --> 00:43:12.780
configure here is an average.

00:43:12.780 --> 00:43:14.790
It's not a total per
CPU, but an average

00:43:14.790 --> 00:43:19.670
calculated of all the CPUs.

00:43:19.670 --> 00:43:23.690
And now going back to my
dashboard, my Custom dashboard,

00:43:23.690 --> 00:43:26.170
so I have all the
use now added here

00:43:26.170 --> 00:43:29.510
with the utilization
on a per CPU basis.

00:43:29.510 --> 00:43:33.480
So it can be monitored,
and all the data.

00:43:33.480 --> 00:43:36.806
And you can also set the
alerts here from Stackdriver.

00:43:44.550 --> 00:43:48.240
So now back to the MIG,
we can see all the MIGs,

00:43:48.240 --> 00:43:50.176
all the servers were created.

00:43:50.176 --> 00:43:52.050
One thing that I would
like to highlight here

00:43:52.050 --> 00:43:53.880
is that I am using
a regional MIG.

00:43:53.880 --> 00:43:58.140
So as you guys can see, there
are two instances per zone--

00:43:58.140 --> 00:44:00.570
two in central B,
two in central 1C.

00:44:00.570 --> 00:44:03.790
So we are providing
zone redundancy as well.

00:44:03.790 --> 00:44:06.144
So traffic is being
distributed in the same region

00:44:06.144 --> 00:44:07.060
across multiple zones.

00:44:19.474 --> 00:44:21.390
Now that's the first
forward that I mentioned.

00:44:21.390 --> 00:44:24.920
If we go back and check
now on the details,

00:44:24.920 --> 00:44:28.440
the auto-scaling is on, but
the number of instances is 1.

00:44:28.440 --> 00:44:30.780
And then they'll
scale down over time

00:44:30.780 --> 00:44:32.610
at the course of
those 10 minutes.

00:44:32.610 --> 00:44:34.620
Its going to decrease
the utilization,

00:44:34.620 --> 00:44:37.740
and then scales
down back to 1 VM,

00:44:37.740 --> 00:44:41.170
as you guys can see
there in the graph.

00:44:41.170 --> 00:44:44.040
Now back to Babi.

00:44:44.040 --> 00:44:46.050
BABI SEAL: So what
this means for you?

00:44:46.050 --> 00:44:47.810
Remember the global network.

00:44:47.810 --> 00:44:50.580
We support resiliency
with live migration.

00:44:50.580 --> 00:44:53.850
And we support scale
and performance.

00:44:53.850 --> 00:44:57.600
We hope we've shown you some
aspects of the Andromeda

00:44:57.600 --> 00:45:01.590
architecture, how it allows
your network services to scale,

00:45:01.590 --> 00:45:05.600
to reliable, and
they're really fast.

00:45:05.600 --> 00:45:09.320
One more thing, we are just
announcing an alpha support

00:45:09.320 --> 00:45:12.140
